 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Input Resolution vs. Window Size:** If the input resolution $(H, W)$ is smaller than or equal to the window size $(W_h, W_w)$, the shift size is forced to $0$ and the window size is reduced to the minimum input dimension to avoid invalid partitioning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** The ray marching implementation in the parent context uses `float` (32-bit) precision. Care must be taken with boundary conditions in the forward projection operator $\mathcal{P}$ to avoid artifacts in the gradient $\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial x}$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** The relative position bias table size grows quadratically with window size. For large windows, this can consume significant memory.
+- **Input Resolution vs Window Size**: If the input resolution ($H$ or $W$) is smaller than or equal to the window size, the shift size is forced to $0$ and the window size is reduced to the minimum input dimension to avoid invalid partitioning [S1, unofficial].
+- **Padding**: Feature maps must be padded to multiples of the window size before partitioning to ensure all pixels are covered by windows [S1, unofficial].
+- **Shifted Windows**: The cyclic shift mechanism requires careful handling of boundary conditions to maintain continuity across window boundaries [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The windowed attention reduces complexity from $O(N^2)$ to $O(N \cdot W_s^2)$, where $N$ is the total number of tokens (pixels/voxels) and $W_s$ is the window size [S1, unofficial]. This makes it significantly faster than global self-attention for high-resolution volumes.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear in the number of tokens $N$, plus the constant overhead of the relative position bias table $O(W_h W_w \cdot num\_heads)$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Designed for sparse-view CT reconstruction, where traditional methods suffer from streak artifacts. DuDoTrans provides high-quality reconstructions by leveraging the dual-domain (sinogram and image) processing capabilities of the transformer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient local attention, ability to capture long-range dependencies via shifted windows, differentiable integration with tomographic reconstruction.
-*   **Limitations:** Performance depends on the quality of the differentiable forward/backward projection operators. Computational cost is higher than non-learning-based iterative methods (e.g., SIRT, SART) per iteration, though convergence may be faster.
+- **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot W_s^2)$ per window, where $N$ is the number of tokens and $W_s$ is the window size. This is significantly faster than global attention $O(N^2)$ for large images [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the number of parameters and the intermediate activations within windows. The relative position bias table size is $O(W_s^2 \cdot \text{num\_heads})$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Efficiently handles large images by limiting attention to local windows while capturing long-range dependencies through shifted windows. Effective for sparse-view CT reconstruction where global context is crucial but computational resources are limited [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Performance may degrade if the window size is not well-tuned for the specific image resolution or feature scale. The cyclic shift operation adds implementation complexity [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use**: Suitable for sparse-view CT reconstruction tasks where high-quality 3D volume reconstruction is required from limited projection data [S1, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use**: Not ideal for real-time applications with strict latency constraints due to the overhead of window partitioning and shifting. Also less effective if the input resolution is very small, as the window size must be reduced [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-DuDoTrans was introduced by Ce Wang, Kun Shang, Haimiao Zhang, Qian Li, and S. Kevin Zhou in the paper "DuDoTrans: Dual-Domain Transformer for Sparse-View CT Reconstruction" [S1, unofficial]. It was published in the International Workshop on Machine Learning for Medical Image Reconstruction in 2022. The method adapts the Swin Transformer architecture, originally designed for natural image processing, to the domain of tomographic reconstruction by incorporating differentiable projection operators [S1, unofficial].
+DuDoTrans was introduced by Ce Wang, Kun Shang, Haimiao Zhang, Qian Li, and S. Kevin Zhou in the paper "DuDoTrans: Dual-Domain Transformer for Sparse-View CT Reconstruction," published in the International Workshop on Machine Learning for Medical Image Reconstruction in 2022 [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader effort to apply deep learning, specifically transformer architectures, to inverse problems in medical imaging [S1, unofficial].
 
